# Build context trim for `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .` (root context, whole reactor).
#
# docker/Dockerfile deliberately does `COPY . .` rather than listing modules: the reactor builds
# service's dependency from source (`configuration` is never published), and a module list would have
# to be edited every time a module is added. This file is what makes that cheap — it drops everything
# the build does not need, so adding a module stays free and no stale output leaks in.

.git
.gitignore

# Maven / Quarkus build output — regenerated inside the build stage, and hundreds of megabytes of it
# here. Copying it in would also risk a stale target/ shadowing what the stage compiles.
**/target
**/target-ide

# The Angular client's install output: the bulk of a working tree once the webui submodule is
# initialised, full of binaries compiled for the HOST rather than for the builder image, and nothing
# in the build stage reads it — that stage runs no install and no client build (see docker/Dockerfile,
# whose three `package-manager-command.*=--version` flags are what neuter them). `.angular` is the
# CLI's own cache, the same shape of host-local junk.
#
# NOTE THE ASYMMETRY with the platform's generic Quinoa recipe, which excludes the client's dist/ as
# well: here dist/ is the PAYLOAD. The client depends on @qits/ui-components, which exists only on
# the platform's own npm registry and is reachable from no address a docker build has, so the bundle
# is built BEFORE this image by the step in .config/qits/ci-post-receive.yml. Excluding dist/ would
# fail the build at the `test -f` guard on the mvnw line.
**/node_modules
service/src/main/webui/.angular

# Quinoa's own toolchain install directory. The build stage creates its own; a host-installed one
# would be a Linux/glibc question we do not want to import from whatever built the context.
**/.quinoa

# Local env / IDE cruft. `.env` is per-machine Quarkus overrides read at ordinal 295 — above the
# packaged application.properties — so shipping one into the build context would let a developer's
# local value quietly outrank what the image is supposed to run with.
.env
.idea
.vscode
.settings
.sdkmanrc

# Docs and the docker/ dir itself: nothing in the build reads them, and keeping them out means
# editing this Dockerfile does not bust the `COPY . .` layer. `maintenance/` is a MODULE, not
# prose — it stays.
*.md
docs
docker
